  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through Monday) \nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nStorms are expected to fire along focusing boundaries (front over\nKS, dryline over the southern High Plains) Tuesday afternoon. This\nactivity will spread east Tuesday night while also forcing the\nfront/outflow down into the forecast area by Wednesday. The CAMs\nare delaying the onset of storm activity into the forecast area\nmore into Wednesday morning compared to some of the global models.\nSome adjustment to PoPs at event onset may be needed with future\nforecast updates. Shower and storm chances ramp up Wednesday with\nthe boundary down into the area. With 70s dewpoints in place in\nthe warm sector, there will be potential for a few strong to\nsevere storms. However, with the main upper system still to the\nwest an organized severe event is not expected.\n\nAnother round of storms is expected Wednesday night into Thursday\nas the system in the Southwest lifts out. This round of storms\nwill be focused SW to NE ahead of the synoptic cold front. Trends\nin the data since yesterday have shifted the heavier QPF axis \nfrom these storms to the west a bit, more noticeable in the \nGFS/GEFS QPF. Nevertheless, the 2 main rounds of rain/storms are \nexpected to drop a good 1 to 2 or 2.5 inches across a good swath \nof northeast Oklahoma and far northwest Arkansas, with the \nheavier amounts the farther west and north you go. Some locally \nheavy amounts in the 4 to 5 inch range or so are possible. A \nFlood Watch might be needed for a portion of northeast Oklahoma in\nfuture forecast updates.\n\nThe synoptic cold front will make a more aggressive push to the\nsoutheast by Thursday night. By this time, the southwest CONUS\nsystem will either have phased with or basically sheared out as it\ngets absorbed into the larger/stronger northern stream trough\ndiving southeast over the Great Lakes. The forcing along the\nboundary collapses and thus not much precip is expected as it\nadvances into SE OK and west-central AR. Much cooler temperatures\nwill filter into the area behind the front for Friday and\nSaturday. In fact, below average temps are expected for a change.\nSome lows in the 30s could happen in the sheltered valleys Friday\nand Saturday night.\n\nA quick warming trend is expected to start the following week,\nwith low chances for rain returning by Monday.\n\nLacy",
